import{_ as n,o as t,c as a,a0 as o}from"./chunks/framework.ChlVJx0I.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Every command line a tool prints is runnable as printed","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/0039-printed-command-lines-are-runnable-as-printed.md","filePath":"adr/0039-printed-command-lines-are-runnable-as-printed.md","lastUpdated":1792092037000}'),s={name:"adr/0039-printed-command-lines-are-runnable-as-printed.md"};function i(r,e,d,c,l,p){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="every-command-line-a-tool-prints-is-runnable-as-printed" tabindex="-1">Every command line a tool prints is runnable as printed <a class="header-anchor" href="#every-command-line-a-tool-prints-is-runnable-as-printed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Every command line a tool prints is runnable as printed&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Issue #135: the sync extension tells a user to run <code>scripts/setup-sync-wizard.sh</code>, a path that is relative to the source checkout. Nobody runs it from the checkout. pi installs a package into a directory the user does not choose and often does not know: a git source is cloned under pi&#39;s git directory, an npm source lands under pi&#39;s npm root, and a local source stays where it is. pi also never links a package&#39;s <code>bin</code> entries onto <code>PATH</code>, so <code>pi-sync</code> is unreachable by name for every managed install, git or npm. A printed path that resolves from no working directory the user is standing in is not a fix; it is a second failure one step later.</p><p><strong>Every command line a tool prints must be pasteable into a shell from any working directory.</strong> That covers error messages, help text, and a wizard&#39;s own closing advice, in the CLI and in the TUI alike. A slash command named in a message (<code>/sync init</code>) already satisfies the rule: it is runnable where it is shown.</p><p><strong>The path is derived from the module&#39;s own location at runtime.</strong> A module knows its own file, so it can walk up to its Install root and name anything the package ships. <code>skill-migrate</code> already derives its root this way. The printed form is absolute and shell-quoted when the path needs quoting: <code>bash &lt;abs&gt;/scripts/setup-sync-wizard.sh</code> for the Account setup script, and <code>node &lt;abs&gt;/extensions/sync/cli.mjs &lt;verb&gt;</code> for the CLI, because a package install can lose the script&#39;s exec bit while <code>bash &lt;path&gt;</code> never cares. A program that prints a path to a file it ships checks that the file is present when it builds the message, and says so plainly when it is not.</p><p><strong>Considered options.</strong> Hardcoding the install path (<code>~/.pi/agent/git/...</code>) is wrong for npm and local installs, and breaks when pi changes its layout or the user relocates the agent directory. Probing <code>PATH</code> for <code>pi-sync</code> and printing the bare name when it resolves adds a moving part that can still be wrong (an alias, a wrapper, a hand-added <code>node_modules/.bin</code>), and prints different text on two machines for no gain. An override environment variable puts the user to work fixing a mistake the tool made. Telling the user to put the bin on <code>PATH</code> is the same mistake, moved.</p><p><strong>Consequences.</strong> Machine-generated command lines are longer than the short <code>pi-sync init</code> spelling, so the docs keep <code>pi-sync</code> as the tool&#39;s name in prose and show the runnable absolute form once, with the docs placeholder <code>&lt;install&gt;</code> defined as the Install root. <code>bash</code> becomes a documented requirement of Account setup: the script is bash, and the fallback advice (<code>PI_SYNC_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID</code>) is already printed beside it. The rule is repo-wide, but this change touches sync only: no other extension prints an unrunnable command line today.</p>',6)])])}const u=n(s,[["render",i]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
